  "primary_subject_agency_ids": [],
  "summary": "ACLU advocacy guide urging communities to oppose Flock and other ALPR deployments, offering tactics including outright rejection or, failing that, contract provisions limiting data retention, sharing, and database use. Cites ICE data sharing, abortion tracking, and Syracuse's cancellation as cautionary examples, and references model contract language drawn from Greensboro NC and other agreements.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "More than 55 localities have ended contracts with Flock in the past year alone.",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "the police department in Syracuse inadvertently shared its local ALPR data via Flock's nationwide system, allowing their data to be searched 4.4 million times in a single year. Syracuse cancelled its Flock contract once this information came to light.",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    },
    {
      "quote": "Flock's default provisions give the company a \"worldwide\" license to use its customers' APLR data.",
      "paragraph_idx": 18
    },
    {
      "quote": "The three most important areas for regulation and negotiation are how long the ALPR data is retained, who the ALPR data is shared with, and how ALPRs and their data are used by law enforcement.",
      "paragraph_idx": 15
    }
  ],
